            %p
              A
              %strong fusion category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+monoidal+category"} rigid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+category"} semisimple
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} linear
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"}> Vect
              \-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"}> enriched
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              (“
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"}> tensor category
              ”), with only
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finitely
              many
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} isomorphism classes
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+objects"} simple objects
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphisms"} endomorphisms
              of the unit object form just the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            Often one also assumes a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braiding
            and speaks of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em braided fusion category
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %p
            The name “fusion category” comes from the central examples of structures whose canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            is called a “fusion product”, notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representations"} representations
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+groups"} loop groups
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebras"} Hopf algebras
            and of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vertex+operator+algebras"} vertex operator algebras
          %p Simple examples:
          %p
          #GradedVectorSpaces.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (graded vector spaces)
              %br/
              For
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                  a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝕂
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
                  a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            %p
              the category of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+spaces"} graded
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi G
                      %mi fdim
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Vect_{G}^{fdim}
            %p
              is a fusion category, with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
              of the group:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi g
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi W
                      %mi h
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi h
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    V_g \otimes W_h
                    \\;\;
                    \\coloneqq
                    \\;\;
                    (V \otimes W)_{g \cdot h}
                    \\,.
            %p More generally, for
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msubsup
                        %mi H
                        %mi Grp
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝕂
                        %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \,\in\, H^3_{Grp}(G, \mathbb{K}^\times)
                a 3-cocycle in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+cohomology"} group cohomology
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+of+units"} group of units
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi 𝕂
                          %mo ×
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}^\times
            %p
              the above construction but with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associator"} associator
              multiplied by the 3-cocycle applied to the
              = succeed "-degrees" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ω
                      %mi fdim
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Vect_{G, \omega}^{fdim}
                    \\,.
            %p
          (
          %a{:href => "#EtingofNikshychOstrik05"}> Etingof, Nikshych &amp; Ostrik 2005, item 1. on p. 584
          )
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
              (or
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "supergroup#finite_supergroups"} finite super-group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/order+of+a+group"} order
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coprime+integer"} relatively prime
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+of+a+field"} characteristic
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝕂
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of representations
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Rep
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep(G, \mathbb{K})
              is a fusion category.
          (
          %a{:href => "#EtingofNikshychOstrik05"}> Etingof, Nikshych &amp; Ostrik 2005, item 2. on p. 584
          )
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_weak_hopf_algebras Relation to weak Hopf algebras
          %p
            Under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            arises as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+category"} representation category
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+Hopf+algebra"} weak Hopf algebra
            (
            %a{:href => "#Ostrik"}> Ostrik
            ). However, this does not mean that every fusion category admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"} fiber functor
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} category of vector spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext Vect
                    %mo =
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Vect}= k-Mod
          %p
            Given any multi-fusion category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi RMod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C\to RMod
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            the algebra spanned by a basis of orthogonal idempotents
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi v
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{v_i\}_{i\in I}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            the equivalence classes of simple objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %em generalized
            fiber functor. The endomorphisms of this functor then give a weak Hopf algebra that represents
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a{:href => "#Hayashi99"} Hayashi 1999
            (see there for the relevant definitions), this is computed as a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\cong Rep(A)
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mtext coend
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ×
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Bmd
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A= \text{coend}(F^*\otimes F: C^{op} \times C \to Bmd(E))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi R
                    %mo ˙
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E=\dot R\otimes R
            is equipped with a coalgebra structure
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ˙
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi ν
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ˙
                  %mi ν
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mover
                    %mi ν
                    %mo ˙
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta(\dot\lambda \mu) = \sum_{\nu\in I} \dot\lambda \nu\otimes \dot\nu \mu
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ˙
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\epsilon (\dot\lambda \mu) = \delta_{\lambda,\mu}
          %p
            It is important to note that, generally speaking,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            may admit other fiber functor to different module categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi RMod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} RMod
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Rep
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Rep(H)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            a Hopf algebra, which admits both the fiber functor described above, as well as a fiber functor to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtext Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{Vect}
          %h3#relation_to_pivotal_and_spherical_categories Relation to pivotal and spherical categories
          %p
            Fusion categories were first systematically studied by
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#EtingofNikshychOstrik05"} Etingof, Nikshych &amp; Ostrik 2005
          .num_theorem
            %h6#conjecture_etingof_nikshych_and_ostrik Conjecture (Etingof, Nikshych, and Ostrik)
            %p
              Every fusion category admits a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+structure"} pivotal structure
          %p
            Providing a certain condition is satisfied, a pivotal structure on a fusion category can be shown to correspond to a ‘twisted’ monoidal natural endotransformation of the identity functor on the category, where the twisting is given by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+symbols"} pivotal symbols
          %h3#tube_ocneanu_algebra Tube (Ocneanu) algebra
          %p
            Every fusion category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tube+algebra"} tube algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            on itself. See there for more.
          %h3#RelationtoTQFT Relation to extended 3d TQFT
          %p
            Given the data of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fusion+category"} fusion category
            one can build a 3d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFT"} extended TQFT
            by various means. This is explained by the fact, see below, that fusion categories are (probably precisely) the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+dualizable+object"} fully dualizable object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi MonCat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            this explains how they induce
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+TQFT"} 3d TQFT
          #MonCat.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi MonCat
                      %mi bim
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat_{bim}
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category"} (infinity,3)-category
              which has as
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  objects
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
              %li
                %p
                  morphism
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodule"} bimodule
              %li
                %p and so on.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              With its natural
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi MonCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28infinity%2Cn%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (infinity,3)-category
          #FusionCategoriesAreFullyDualizable.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              A monoidal category which is fusion is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+dualizable+object"} fully dualizable
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2Cn%29-category"} (infinity,3)-category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi MonCat
                        %mi bim
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} MonCat_{bim}
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MonCat"}
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#DSPS13"}> Douglas &amp; Schommer-Pries &amp; Snyder 13
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Via the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theorem"} cobordism theorem
              prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FusionCategoriesAreFullyDualizable"}
              implies that fusion categories encode
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+TQFTs"} extended TQFTs
              on 3-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+manifold"} framed
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi O
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+fixed+points"} homotopy fixed points
              encode extended 3d TQFTs on general (not framed) cobordisms.
            %p
              These 3d TQFTs hence arise from similar algebraic data as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Turaev-Viro+model"} Turaev-Viro model
              and the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reshetikhin-Turaev+construction"} Reshetikhin-Turaev construction
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#DSPS13"} Douglas &amp; Schommer-Pries &amp; Snyder 13, p. 5
